= Bifilar Coil Engineering =

{{Plain_Hook
| in_one_sentence    = The bifilar coil is the unique coil geometry that simultaneously maximises the local electric field squared (which enters the <math>\alpha F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}</math> ψ coupling term) while cancelling the far-field magnetic field to negligible levels — making it both the most efficient [[ψ Transducer]] (C3) geometry and the only geometry that satisfies ICNIRP 2010 reference levels for a head-worn drive system at 200V.
| everyday_analogy   = A balanced audio cable carries the signal on two conductors with equal and opposite voltages — not because it sounds better, but because external noise fields that couple identically to both conductors cancel out at the differential receiver. A bifilar coil works by the same principle in reverse: the two traces carry opposite currents, so their magnetic fields cancel in the far field. But the electric field between the traces is additive rather than cancelling — concentrated in the inter-trace gap at twice the voltage gradient of a single trace.
| what_it_predicts   = (1) A peak inter-trace E-field of ~1 MV/m at 200V drive across a 0.2 mm gap — the field that drives the BST composite's <math>\varepsilon_{\rm eff}(V)</math> and sources ψ via the coupling term; (2) A far-field E of ~1 V/m at 10 cm — well below the ICNIRP 2010 limit of 83 V/m at 40 kHz; (3) A near-zero far-field B from bifilar cancellation, confirmed by the ratio <math>B_{\rm far}/B_{\rm near} \propto (d_{\rm gap}/r)^2</math>; (4) A peak drive current of ~100A at 200V through 2Ω trace resistance — requiring pulsed operation at < 0.1% duty cycle for thermal management.
| if_you_remember_one_thing = The 0.2 mm gap is not an arbitrary dimension — it is derived from the requirement to achieve E-field = 1 MV/m at 200V (which requires exactly 0.2 mm) while maintaining a gap large enough for the 50 μm dielectric overcoat to provide reliable electrical insulation at 200V with a factor-of-2.5 safety margin. Every bifilar trace dimension traces to a physical requirement.
}}

The bifilar coil is the electromagnetic foundation of every [[Psi-Tech]] module. It is where the conventional engineering world — voltages, currents, switching transistors — connects to the psionic field via the <math>\alpha F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}</math> coupling term established in [[Psionics]] and formalised in [[ψ Transducer]] (C3). This page specifies every engineering dimension of the coil system, from trace geometry through winding pattern to fabrication protocol and compliance verification.

== Why Bifilar: The Quantitative Argument ==

=== The Goal: Maximise F²μν, Satisfy ICNIRP ===

The EM-to-ψ coupling source term is:

:<math>S_{\rm EM\to\psi}(\mathbf{x},t) = \alpha\,F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu} = 2\alpha\left(B^2 - \frac{E^2}{c^2}\right)</math>

In natural units: <math>F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu} = 2(B^2 - E^2)</math>. To maximise <math>|S|</math>, maximise <math>|B^2 - E^2|</math>. Two strategies:

* Strategy A: Large B, small E → possible with a solenoid, but violates ICNIRP B-field limit (27 μT at 10 cm for 40 kHz) at any drive current producing useful field
* Strategy B: Large E, zero B → achievable only with bifilar geometry; E-field drives ψ source, B cancels in far field

Strategy B is the only viable option for a head-worn device. The bifilar geometry is not a preference — it is the physically forced choice given ICNIRP constraints.

=== The Cancellation Factor ===

Two parallel traces separated by <math>d_{\rm gap}</math>, each carrying current <math>I</math> in opposite directions, produce far-field magnetic fields that partially cancel. At observation distance <math>r \gg d_{\rm gap}</math>, the residual far-field B:

:<math>B_{\rm far}(r) \approx \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi}\cdot\frac{d_{\rm gap}}{r^2} \qquad (r \gg d_{\rm gap})</math>

The cancellation factor relative to a single-trace coil at the same current:

:<math>\frac{B_{\rm far}^{\rm bifilar}}{B_{\rm far}^{\rm single}} \approx \frac{d_{\rm gap}}{r}</math>

At <math>d_{\rm gap} = 0.2</math> mm and <math>r = 100</math> mm (ICNIRP measurement point):

:<math>\text{Cancellation} = \frac{0.2}{100} = 2\times10^{-3}</math>

The bifilar geometry reduces far-field B by a factor of 500 relative to a single-trace coil at the same drive current. This is what makes ICNIRP compliance achievable.

== E-Field Geometry and Specifications ==

=== Inter-Trace E-Field ===

For two parallel traces of width <math>w_{\rm trace} = 0.3</math> mm separated by gap <math>d_{\rm gap} = 0.2</math> mm, driven at differential voltage <math>V_{\rm drive}</math>, the peak E-field in the gap (parallel plate approximation, valid for <math>w_{\rm trace} \gg d_{\rm gap}</math>):

:<math>E_{\rm peak} = \frac{V_{\rm drive}}{d_{\rm gap}}</math>

At <math>V_{\rm drive} = 200</math> V:

:<math>E_{\rm peak} = \frac{200}{2\times10^{-4}} = 10^6\,\text{V/m} = 1\,\text{MV/m}</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = Peak inter-trace electric field
| equation       = <math>E_{\rm peak} = V_{\rm drive}/d_{\rm gap} = 200\,\text{V}/0.2\,\text{mm} = 1\,\text{MV/m}</math>
| in_english     =
* This field is concentrated entirely in the 0.2 mm gap between the traces — within the BST composite fill of the TPMS lattice.
* Outside the gap, the field falls as <math>E(r) \approx E_{\rm peak}\,(d_{\rm gap}/2r)^2</math> for <math>r \gg d_{\rm gap}</math>.
* At <math>r = 1</math> mm: <math>E \approx 10^6 \times (0.1)^2 = 10^4</math> V/m — still large, but now outside the composite volume.
* At <math>r = 100</math> mm: <math>E \approx 10^6 \times (0.001)^2 \approx 1</math> V/m — well below ICNIRP.
* The field is both intense (for transduction) and tightly localised (for safety). This spatial separation of near and far field is the engineering foundation of the bifilar design.
}}

