== ICNIRP 2010 Compliance Assessment ==

ICNIRP (International Commission on Non-Ionizing Radiation Protection) 2010 reference levels for occupational exposure at 40 kHz:
* E-field: 83 V/m (head-worn: general public limit applies; use 83 V/m)
* H-field: 21 A/m (equivalent to B = 27 μT)

=== E-Field Assessment ===

From the falloff table above: far-field E at 10 cm = ~1 V/m < 83 V/m. ✓

Measurement point: 10 cm from the nearest bifilar trace. For a head-worn shell with bifilar traces on the shell surface, the nearest external point at 10 cm is approximately at the cheek surface when the device is worn. The calculated 1 V/m provides an 83× safety margin.

=== B-Field Assessment ===

Peak current in each bifilar trace at 200V drive through 2Ω trace resistance: <math>I_{\rm peak} = 200/2 = 100</math> A.

Near-field B from a single trace at 10 cm: <math>B_{\rm single} = \mu_0 I/(2\pi r) = 4\pi\times10^{-7} \times 100/(2\pi \times 0.1) = 200\,\mu\text{T}</math>

With bifilar cancellation factor 500 (from Section 1):

:<math>B_{\rm far}^{\rm bifilar} = 200/500 = 0.4\,\mu\text{T} < 27\,\mu\text{T}\,\checkmark</math>

At 200V, 100A peak, the bifilar shell produces far-field B = 0.4 μT at 10 cm — 67× below the ICNIRP reference level. ✓

=== Pulsed Drive Constraint ===

The 100A peak current through 2Ω trace resistance dissipates <math>P_{\rm peak} = I^2 R = 100^2 \times 2 = 20</math> kW instantaneously. For continuous operation, this is impossible for a wearable device. Pulsed operation is required.

'''Adiabatic pulse condition:''' For pulse duration <math>\tau_{\rm pulse} \ll \tau_{\rm thermal}</math> (the thermal time constant of the trace), the temperature rise per pulse is:

:<math>\Delta T_{\rm pulse} = \frac{P_{\rm peak}\,\tau_{\rm pulse}}{\rho c_p V_{\rm trace}}</math>

For 2 μm copper trace, 0.3 mm wide, 1 mm long: <math>V_{\rm trace} = 6\times10^{-13}</math> m³; <math>\rho c_p = 3.44\times10^6</math> J/(m³·K):

At <math>\tau_{\rm pulse} = 1\,\mu</math>s: <math>\Delta T = 20000 \times 10^{-6}/(3.44\times10^6 \times 6\times10^{-13}) = 9.7</math>°C per pulse.

At 0.1% duty cycle (1 μs on, 1 ms off): average dissipation = 20 W × 0.001 = 20 mW per metre of trace — trivially manageable. The adiabatic pulse condition is satisfied: the trace heats by 9.7°C per pulse, then cools almost completely during the 1 ms off-period. Trace lifetime: > 10⁹ cycles at this duty cycle (copper fatigue lifetime well-established for this thermal excursion amplitude).

